.TH WRIT\-INIT 1 2026-10-12 "writ 0.1.0" "writ manual"
.SH NAME
writ\-init \- create an identity
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nh
.ad l
\fBwrit init\fR \fB\-\-next\-out\fR\ \fIPATH\fR [\fB\-\-next\-n\fR\ \fIN\fR] [\fB\-\-next\-k\fR\ \fIK\fR] [\fB\-\-next\-pub\fR\ \fIPATH\fR]... [\fB\-\-key\-out\fR\ \fIPATH\fR] [\fB\-\-max\-validity\fR\ \fIDUR\fR] [\fB\-q\fR] \fIDIR\fR
.ad
.hy
.SH DESCRIPTION
Create a new identity in DIR and print its id.
.PP
An identity has two kinds of keys. The current key signs statements. writ writes it to DIR/current.key. The next key can only rotate the identity: replace its keys and keep its id. Keep the next key offline. You must say where it goes with \-\-next\-out.
.PP
Key files are plain text. writ never overwrites a file, and creates key files that only you can read. To encrypt keys, use age(1):
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
writ init me \-\-next\-out \- | age \-p > me\-next.key.age
.fi
.RE
.PP
The next keys can be K of N keys held by different people. For example, 2 of 3: your own offline key, a friend's key, and a key in a safe. K must be more than half of N, so that two separate groups of holders can't each rotate.
.SH ARGUMENTS
.TP
\fIDIR\fR
the directory to create; it must not exist, or must be empty
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
\fB\-\-next\-out\fR \fIPATH\fR
where to write the next key, outside DIR; '\-' means standard output
.TP
\fB\-\-next\-n\fR \fIN\fR
how many new next keys to make (default: 1)
.TP
\fB\-\-next\-k\fR \fIK\fR
how many next keys must sign a rotation (default: more than half of them)
.TP
\fB\-\-next\-pub\fR \fIPATH\fR
add another person's public key (from \fBwrit pubkey\fR) to the next keys
.TP
\fB\-\-key\-out\fR \fIPATH\fR
where to write the current key (default: DIR/current.key)
.TP
\fB\-\-max\-validity\fR \fIDUR\fR
require every statement to expire within DUR, such as 90d
.TP
\fB\-q\fR, \fB\-\-quiet\fR
print only the id
.TP
\fB\-h\fR, \fB\-\-help\fR
print help; \fB\-h\fR is short, \fB\-\-help\fR is long
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
0
success; for questions, the answer is yes
.TP
1
the answer is no: the check, verification, proof or name lookup was denied
.TP
2
an error: bad usage, unreadable input, or a failed operation
.SH EXAMPLES
Create an identity, with the next key encrypted by age:
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
writ init me \-\-next\-out \- | age \-p > me\-next.key.age
.fi
.RE
.PP
Next keys held by three people, any two of whom can rotate:
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
writ init org \-\-next\-out mine.key \-\-next\-pub alice.pub \-\-next\-pub bob.pub \-\-next\-k 2
.fi
.RE
.SH SEE ALSO
\fBwrit\fR(1), \fBwrit\-rotate\fR(1), \fBwrit\-keygen\fR(1), \fBwrit\-pubkey\fR(1)
